## Budget / stop (machine-readable — the runtime enforces this)

These limits are **hard stops**, not suggestions. When any limit is reached, the
loop halts immediately, writes a `budget-exceeded` status to `{{STATE_PATH}}`,
and waits for the student to raise the budget or mark the loop done.

Set at least one of the three keys. An unset key is ignored (no limit on that
dimension), so leaving all three blank means **no budget** — which is not
allowed. Fill in concrete numbers at scaffold time.

```loop-budget
max_iterations: {{MAX_ITERATIONS}}
max_tokens: {{MAX_TOKENS}}
max_minutes: {{MAX_MINUTES}}
```

- `max_iterations` — stop after this many iterations.
- `max_tokens` — stop once cumulative tokens spent by the loop reach this.
- `max_minutes` — stop once wall-clock time across the run reaches this.

### Why a hard stop is required

An agent loop cold-starts on every run with no inherent awareness of how many
times it has already fired. Without an external counter in `{{STATE_PATH}}` and a
ceiling checked against it, nothing prevents the loop from running indefinitely —
draining the student's token usage. The `loop-budget` block is the brake the
runtime checks before every iteration; because it lives on disk it survives the
cold start that an in-memory counter would not.
